@ Index:   Propositional logic: tautology, contradiction or contingency (the whole truth table at once)
@ Author:  Victor Valdes
@ Version: 0.1.5
@ Source:  https://github.com/valdesvj/logic47
@ Tested:  on the C47 by the author (Oct 2026)
@ Tested:  C47 simulator (python/c47sim.py): tests/test_logic47.py, 39 fixed formulas and 317 random ones of
@ Tested:  1-6 variables against a separate Python evaluator
@ Input:   the formula, keyed with F1-F6 on three pages (1 p q r s t u, 2 the connectives, 3 ( ) T F)
@ Output:  p->q (keys F1, 2 F4, 1 F2, 5): CONTINGENCY, true in 3 of 4 rows, T: p=0 q=0  F: p=1 q=0
@
@ Logic 47: propositional logic on the C47 / R47. Variables p q r s t u, the constants true and false,
@ not, and, or, exclusive or, implies, if and only if, brackets. 5 evaluates the formula: TAUTOLOGY,
@ CONTRADICTION or CONTINGENCY, the number of true rows, and for a contingency a true and a false row.
@ Each variable is a 64-bit word (one bit a row), so the formula is evaluated once with AND OR XOR NOT.
@
@ Keys: F1-F6 a symbol, 1 2 3 the page, 4 delete, 5 evaluate, 7 clear, 0 the end.
@ Your stack and LASTx are kept (STOS / RCLS in local registers); the registers R20-R95 are cleared at the end.
@ Needs a C47 / R47 firmware with ATEXT, GRFNT, the STRUCT commands and the bitwise commands (WSIZE 64).
@ The comments after ';' say what each part does (rejig drops them: they take no memory).
@
@ LOGIC47 v0.1.5 - Logic 47: propositional logic on the C47 / R47
@ A formula of up to six variables p q r s t u, the constants ⊤ ⊥ (drawn T ⊥), the connectives ¬ ∧ ∨ ⊕ (drawn ⊻) → ↔, brackets,
@ keyed with the soft keys F1-F6 (three pages: 1 the variables, 2 the connectives, 3 the brackets and
@ constants). 5 evaluates it: TAUTOLOGY, CONTRADICTION or CONTINGENCY, the number of rows where it is
@ true, and for a contingency a row where it is true and one where it is false.
@ The whole truth table at once: each variable is a 64-bit word, bit k = its value in row k (p = ...1010,
@ q = ...1100, r = ...11110000, ... u = 32 ones then 32 zeros), so the formula evaluated once with AND, OR,
@ XOR and NOT gives the word of its 64 rows. A variable not in the formula only repeats rows: the rows are
@ 2^n for the n variables used, and the true rows the set bits / 2^(6 - n).
@ Precedence, high to low: ¬, ∧, ∨ and ⊕, →, ↔ (→ to the right: p→q→r = p→(q→r); the others to the left).
@ Keys: F1-F6 a symbol of the page, 1 2 3 the page, 4 delete the last symbol, 5 evaluate, 7 clear, 0 end.
@ The user's stack and LASTx are kept; at the end R20-R95 are cleared, WSIZE stays 64.
@ Screen 400 x 240, row 0 = bottom. ATEXT in GRFNT 21 (GRFNT 10 for the small text).
@ Registers: R20 the page, R21 the formula (">" then its symbols), R22 its number of symbols, R23 the key,
@ R24 the result word, R25-R32 the words of p q r s t u ⊤ ⊥ (R25 + the code), R33 the symbol being read,
@ R34 the text, R35 the value stack pointer (values R50-R69), R36 the operator stack pointer (R70-R89),
@ R37 1: syntax error, R38 the code, R39-R45 work, R46 the code letters "abcdefghijklmnop" (code = place),
@ R47 the true rows, R48 a true row, R49 a false row, R90-R92 the pages' code letters, R93 the variables'
@ names, R94-R95 the formula's glyphs. R21 holds ">" and the code letters (ASCII), not the glyphs: on the C47 a
@ glyph such as ¬ may take two bytes, and αPOS, αSL, αLEFT would then not count it as one character.
@ Control flow in the C47 STRUCT commands (IF / ELSE / ENDIF, DO / WHILE / ENDDO), indented two spaces per
@ level; tools/build_logic47.py checks them and numbers them.
@

